  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1032 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nKDDC WSR-88D shows that the far southeast counties have\nreceived the most radar estimated rainfall amounts. This \nmatches with local observations down by Medicine Lodge, showing \nthe highest amounts. Showers will continue through the morning \nhours. Areal coverage should diminish by sunrise. Otherwise, \nmuch of the day should be dry. Mild highs are expected today \nwith values peaking in the upper 50s to lower 60s. The NBM is \ncranking out pops across the eastern zones this afternoon. \nSeveral CAMs, however, are not that impressive with any \nadditional shower activity. In general, Trick-or-Treat time \nshould be generally dry. Temperatures in the evening will be \nmild with a range of 50s in the north to 60s in the south. Winds\nwill be slightly stronger today with a breezy SE to S wind 12 \nto 22 mph. Lows tonight will range from the 40s in the west to \nthe 50s in the east.\n\nFor Sunday, models are indicating shortwave ridging developing\nacross the region. This ridging is upstream of a large open\nupper level trof that will move across the western United States\nduring the daytime hours. This ridging will support the notion\nof a drier forecast and warmer highs. Highs should peak from the\nmid to upper 60s in the north to the lower 70s in the south.\nAnother day of breezy SE to S winds are expected as we close out\nthe weekend.\n\nLong range models and their ensemble counterparts are still\nindicating the passage of an open and progressive wave Monday\nevening into Tuesday morning. The sfc low among several \nensemble members is displaced well to our north. This quick \nhitting system with the low well to our north tends to favor \nlight precipitation amounts and the heavier precipitation axis \ndisplaced well to the east of southwest Kansas. There should be \nsome cyclogenesis with the sfc low during the day, so the \noverall windfield and pressure gradient should be more \nimpressive than what we have seen as of late. Gusty southerly \nwinds are expected prefrontal with gusty northerly winds \npostfrontal. SPC has an area outlined Monday, but it looks like \nthe brunt of the warm sector and related instability will be \neast/southeast of the FA.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, ensembles are forecasting more shortwave ridging\nWednesday with a drier forecast and a continuation of mild\ntemperatures. The uneventful weather pattern looks to continue\nfor the rest of the business week with generally dry conditions\nand mild temperatures.",
